---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';

const page = codexPage('byzantine-fault-tolerance');
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>
    A federation is a few computers that must behave like one. If one of them crashes, the others can carry on without it. The
    harder case is a member that keeps running but sends wrong data, or tells different peers different stories. That is a
    <strong>Byzantine fault</strong>, and a system that keeps working through it is <strong>Byzantine fault tolerant</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>The generals and the traitors</h2>
  <p>
    The name comes from a 1982 paper by Lamport, Shostak and Pease.<Cite page={page} s="lsp" /> Several generals surround a city
    and must agree to attack or retreat, but they can only talk through messengers, and some generals are traitors who send
    conflicting orders. The paper shows that with ordinary messages, the loyal generals can always agree only if more than two
    thirds of the generals are loyal: tolerating <em>m</em> traitors takes at least 3<em>m</em> + 1 generals.
  </p>

  <h2>From a thought experiment to real systems</h2>
  <p>
    In 1999 Castro and Liskov showed that a replicated service can tolerate <em>f</em> faulty replicas out of 3<em>f</em> + 1
    and still run fast enough for real use.<Cite page={page} s="pbft" /> Their protocol promises two things: <strong>safety</strong>,
    meaning every honest replica executes the same requests in the same order, and <strong>liveness</strong>, meaning requests
    eventually complete.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fedimint guardians do the same job. They run a consensus protocol to agree on one ordered list of transactions, then each
    guardian applies that list to its own copy of the state, so they all end up in the same place.<Cite page={page} s="fmArchitecture" />
    The consensus layer is AlephBFT, an asynchronous Byzantine fault tolerant protocol.<Cite page={page} s="aleph" />
  </p>

  <h2>When there are too many traitors</h2>
  <p>
    The guarantees hold only while at most <em>f</em> members are faulty. Past that point the federation may stop making progress,
    and a large enough group acting together could approve something the honest guardians never would. How big <em>f</em> can
    be for a given federation is the subject of <a href="/codex/one-third-rule/">the one-third rule</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How the game models it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Byzantine guardians look exactly like honest ones. Only the server knows the roles until the match ends.</li>
    <li>They forge withdrawals and try to collect enough signatures for them, the way a faulty member pushes a bad transaction.</li>
    <li>Honest guardians work from evidence: private audits, task results, who was where. Then they decide in a council whether to rotate a suspect’s key out.</li>
    <li>Rotating out an honest guardian by mistake shrinks the federation and brings the Byzantines closer to a third.</li>
  </ul>
</Article>
